module dev.slatelang.slate

// The TAG every builtin is dispatched on: one enum arm per function a program has without writing
// it.
//
// **Cut out of `value.sysl` when that file passed a thousand lines**, and it is the one seam that
// file has: everything else there is what a slate program computes with -- the `Value` union, a
// scope, the tags an object carries -- and this is a closed list of names, five hundred lines of
// it, that happens to be reached from a `Value`'s `Native` arm.
//
// **Three files say "the builtins" and they say three different things.** This one is the NAME,
// `natives.sysl` is which names a scope gets and which a built-in module has taken over, and
// `builtin.sysl` is `call_native` and the `do_*` functions behind it. Nothing here is `private` and
// nothing here may become private: every one of the other two reads these arms by name.

// The functions a program has without writing them.
enum NativeFn derives Display
    NPrint
    NAssert
    NAssertEq

    // **A call that is supposed to raise**, which is the one assertion that cannot be written as a
    // condition -- a fault leaves the test rather than answering false.
    NAssertFaults

    // **The third thing a `@test` may say**, beside the two assertions: that it is not going to run
    // here at all, and why.
    NSkip

    // **Which of the three hosts this program is running on** -- `"interpreter"`, `"node"` or
    // `"browser"`. Declared into a program's own scope, unlike `NHostHas` below: a component that
    // runs on any of the three is the one thing that needs to ask.
    NHost
    NLen

    // -- text --
    NChars
    NSplit
    NJoin
    NContains
    NIndexOf
    NLastIndexOf
    NStartsWith
    NEndsWith
    NTrim
    NTrimStart
    NTrimEnd
    NUpper
    NLower

    // **The two Unicode names that are not a case mapping.** `normalize` is which of the four ways
    // one string may be written, and `casefold` is what two strings differing only in case both
    // come to -- which is not `lower`, `ß` folding to `ss` where lowering leaves it.
    NNormalize
    NCasefold
    NReplace
    NRepeat

    // **Padding to a WIDTH, which is what a column of text is made of.** The width is counted in
    // characters like every other position here, so a name with an accent in it lines up with one
    // without.
    NPadStart
    NPadEnd

    // -- numbers --
    NNumber
    NInteger
    NReal
    NBoolean
    NAbs
    NFloor
    NCeil
    NRound
    NTrunc
    NSqrt
    NPow
    NMin
    NMax

    // **The two that render a number for a PERSON to read.** `string` answers six significant digits
    // because that is what `%g` does, which is right for a diagnostic and wrong for a price;
    // `toFixed` says how many places, and `formatNumber` groups the thousands.
    NToFixed
    NFormatNumber
    NPush
    NString
    NKeys
    NParseJson
    NToJson
    NToBytes
    NFromBytes
    NValues
    NEntries
    NHas

    // **`without` and not `remove`**, which `slate:fs` took for deleting a file. An object is not
    // changed, so the participle is right anyway: `sorted` answers a new array and this answers a
    // new object.
    NWithout

    // -- the object model every value answers to --
    //
    // **These four are the universal methods**, reached on any value that has not put a field of the
    // same name in front of them. `method.sysl` says why they are the only names allowed to do that.
    NToString
    NEq
    NNe
    NEquals

    // -- arrays --
    //
    // **The participle answers a new array and the bare verb changes the one it was given**, which
    // is Python's convention and is what makes a mutation visible at the call site. `array.sysl`
    // holds the rest of the rule.
    NPop
    NInsert
    NRemoveAt
    NClear
    NMap
    NFilter
    NReduce
    NFind
    NFindIndex
    NEvery
    NSome
    NSort
    NSorted
    NReverse
    NReversed
    NConcat
    NFlat
    NSum

    // The eight an array answers that it did not before. **`at` and `slice` count from the END where
    // the index is negative**, which is the whole reason JavaScript grew `at` beside `xs[i]`.
    NSlice
    NShift
    NUnshift
    NFlatMap
    NForEach
    NFindLast
    NFindLastIndex

    // **The eight a program writes a loop for when they are not there.** Every one answers a NEW
    // array or object and changes nothing, so none of them is a bare verb: `groupBy` and `partition`
    // cut an array up, `zip`, `chunk` and `unique` reshape it, and `count`, `minBy` and `maxBy` ask
    // one question about the whole of it.
    NGroupBy
    NZip
    NUnique
    NChunk
    NCount
    NPartition
    NMinBy
    NMaxBy

    // The event loop's, which are node's names because a program that knows node should not have to
    // learn new ones for the same thing.
    NSetTimeout
    NSetInterval
    NClearTimeout

    // Promises. `sleep` is the one that makes `await` worth having on its own -- everything else a
    // program can build out of an async function.
    NSleep
    NResolve
    NReject

    // **The four that wait on MANY promises at once**, which is the one thing `await` cannot be
    // written to do: awaiting in sequence starts the second only once the first is done. They are
    // JavaScript's `Promise.all`, `.allSettled`, `.race` and `.any` as bare names, slate having no
    // namespaces.
    NAll
    NAllSettled
    NRace
    NAny

    // The file system. **node's names**, and every one of them answers a promise -- there is no
    // blocking form, which is the one place slate is stricter than node rather than looser.
    NReadFile
    NReadBytes
    NWriteFile
    NAppendFile

    // **Bytes on the writing side, which is the reading `send` already had.** `writeFile` renders
    // anything that is not a string the way `print` does, so an array reached the disk as its own
    // text -- and a program holding an image or a compressed body had to encode it to store it.
    NWriteBytes
    NAppendBytes
    NStderr
    NReadDir
    NStat
    NExists
    NRemove
    NMkdir
    NRmdir
    NRename

    // The same ten, blocking. **`Sync` is node's suffix and the reason for having them is node's
    // too**: a program that reads its configuration before it starts, or a script whose whole job is
    // three file operations in order, gains nothing from a promise and pays for it in ceremony.
    NReadFileSync
    NReadBytesSync
    NWriteFileSync
    NAppendFileSync
    NWriteBytesSync
    NAppendBytesSync
    NReadDirSync
    NStatSync
    NExistsSync
    NRemoveSync
    NMkdirSync
    NRmdirSync
    NRenameSync

    // TCP. **A socket is a handle with a lifetime**, which is what makes this different from the
    // file system: `connect` and `send` answer promises, and `listen` and `onData` take callbacks,
    // because a stream is a run of events rather than one answer.
    NListen
    NConnect
    NOnData
    NOnBytes
    NOnError
    NSend
    NCloseSocket
    NLocalPort

    // **Who is at the other end**, which a server has no other way to learn -- `slate:http` told a
    // handler the method, the path and the headers and nothing at all about the client.
    NRemoteAddress
    NStartTls

    // **Which application protocol a TLS connection settled on**, which is the one question a server
    // taking both HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 on one port cannot answer any other way.
    NAlpnProtocol

    // Dates and times. **One word per question, and the receiver says which question it is.**
    // `2.hours()` makes a length and `d.hours()` asks how many whole hours a length is; `date(y, m,
    // d)` builds a calendar day and `date(z)` reads one off a zoned reading. That is the same rule
    // `method.sysl` already runs on -- a builtin method is the free function with the receiver in
    // front -- applied to a vocabulary where the noun and the verb are the same word in English too.
    NNow
    NMonotonic
    NDate
    NTime
    NDateTime
    NZone
    NLocalZone
    NInstant
    NEpochSeconds
    NEpochMillis
    NEpochMicros

    // The exact lengths and the calendar ones, which are two scales rather than one. Nothing here
    // converts between them, because a month is not a number of days and a day is not a number of
    // hours.
    NMicros
    NMillis
    NSeconds
    NMinutes
    NHours
    NDays
    NWeeks
    NMonths
    NYears

    // What a reading is made of. Singular where the length words are plural, which is the whole of
    // how `year(d)` is told from `years(3)`.
    NYear
    NMonth
    NDay
    NHour
    NMinute
    NSecond
    NWeekday
    NMonthName
    NDayOfYear
    NDaysInMonth
    NIsLeapYear
    NAt
    NOffset
    NIsDst
    NAbbrev
    NStartOf
    NOnOrAfter
    NFormat
    NParseDate
    NParseTime
    NParseDateTime
    NParseTimestamp

    // Another program, run and read. **On libuv like every other host binding here**, slate being an
    // asynchronous language and `sysl.process` being blocking throughout.
    NRun
    NEnv

    // A child that outlives the call that started it, and the message channel it was given. **The
    // methods are natives without global names**, exactly as a set's and a map's are: `send`, `kill`
    // and `pid` are words a program wants for its own, and a child answers them because of what it
    // is rather than because slate took the names.
    NSpawn
    NChannel
    NChildSend
    NChildKill
    NChildOnMessage
    NChildOnExit
    NChildPid
    NChildExited
    NChannelSend
    NChannelOnMessage

    // What the machine and the command line say about this program itself: how many workers are
    // worth running, and what would have to be started to run this program again. **`execPath` and
    // `scriptPath` are declared into a built-in module's scope and into no program's**, `hostHas`'s
    // rule and for its reason: they exist so that `slate:cluster` can start a copy of the program
    // it is running inside, which is a module's business and not a vocabulary a program wants.
    NCpus
    NExecPath
    NScriptPath

    // Stopping the program with a status of the program's own choosing. **`args` is beside it in
    // `slate:process` and is not here**, being a value rather than a call: the arguments are fixed
    // before the first statement runs, so asking for them cannot fail and cannot change its answer.
    NExit

    // Being asked to stop by something outside the program, which is the other half of `exit` and
    // the only way a server ever ends. In `slate:process` for the same reason `exit` is: what this
    // process is told and what it tells the shell are one subject.
    NOnSignal
    NOffSignal

    // A generator, driven a step at a time. `for x in g` is the other way to drive one and needs no
    // builtin -- `IterNext` asks the same question the loop already asks of an array.
    NGenNext

    // An HTTP server, on the event loop slate already has, and the request a program makes itself.
    NFetch

    // **A promise nobody has answered yet, and the two ways of answering it.** `resolve` and
    // `reject` make a promise that has already settled, which is the easy half; these are the half
    // that bridges a callback into `await`, and without them a program can only wait for something
    // slate itself started. TC39's `Promise.withResolvers` is the same three pieces.
    NPending
    NSettle
    NFail

    // **The parser `slate:http` reads bytes with, and `slate:llhttp` is the door to it.** They are
    // declared inside `if everything`, so they are not in a program's own scope -- but the export
    // list in `stdlib.sysl` hands them out, which is what lets a proxy, a client or a protocol that
    // starts as HTTP be written as a package rather than as a change to slate.
    NHttpParser
    NHttpStream
    NHttpFeed
    NHttpTake
    NHttpTakePart
    NHttpFinish
    NHttpClose
    NHttpUpgraded

    // **HTTP/2, which is `slate:nghttp2` and is nghttp2 underneath.** The sibling of the eight above and
    // public for their reason: this is the framing and header-compression layer, and what is written
    // over it -- a server taking both versions on one port, a client that keeps its connections, a
    // proxy, gRPC -- is a package rather than a change to slate.
    NH2Client
    NH2Server
    NH2Receive
    NH2Send
    NH2Next
    NH2Request
    NH2Respond

    // The same two with the body left open, and the two that write it. **A streamed body is four
    // names rather than a flag on the other two**, because what a caller does afterwards is
    // different: it holds the stream and writes to it, where `h2Respond` is finished when it
    // returns.
    NH2RequestStream
    NH2RespondStream
    NH2Push
    NH2Finish

    NH2Settings
    NH2Goaway
    NH2Reset
    NH2WindowUpdate
    NH2Consume
    NH2Ping
    NH2Wants
    NH2Close

    // **HPACK on its own, which needs no session at all.** It is the security-relevant half of
    // HTTP/2 and is identical whoever drives the frames, so a program holding a header block from
    // anywhere -- a capture, a test vector, a proxy's log -- can read it without one.
    NHpackDeflater
    NHpackInflater
    NHpackDeflate
    NHpackInflate
    NHpackClose

    // **What `slate:jwt` signs and checks with**, and inside `if everything` for the same reason the
    // parser is: a token's shape is slate's business and the cryptography is not part of the
    // language.
    NJwsSign
    NJwsVerify

    // **What `slate:crypto` hands a PROGRAM**, which is a different question from what the two
    // modules above stand on: a package cannot have a native, so anything installed with `slate add`
    // had no digest and -- the half that cannot be written in slate at any price -- no randomness.
    // `NSha1` is in both lists, `slate:ws` reaching it from the builtin scope and a program by name.
    NMd5
    NSha1
    NSha256
    NSha384
    NSha512
    NHmac
    NPbkdf2
    NRandomBytes
    NTimingSafeEqual

    // **Argon2id, which is `slate:crypto`'s password hash.** Beside the digests because they are one
    // module, and named for the algorithm for that module's reason: a `hash` meaning the slow one
    // next to `sha256` meaning a fast one is the confusion a login path must not have.
    NArgon2
    NArgon2Verify
    NArgon2NeedsRehash

    // **Brotli, which `slate:brotli` exports and `slate:http` uses without being asked.** Declared
    // like the Argon2 natives rather than inside `if everything`: they ARE the module, and the
    // server reaching for them is a second consumer of the same two names rather than a seam.
    NBrotliCompress
    NBrotliDecompress

    // **gzip and zlib, which are `slate:gzip`.** Beside brotli rather than inside `if everything`
    // for its reason -- these four ARE the module. What separates them from brotli is that a
    // JavaScript host has them: `CompressionStream` speaks both wrappers and speaks no brotli, so
    // this is the compression a program has wherever slate runs.
    // **Zstandard, whose names are the format's own verbs.** `compress` and `decompress` are
    // `slate:brotli`'s and every built-in module's names go into one scope, so gzip's rule decides
    // this one too: a module is named for what the command line calls it.
    NZstd
    NUnzstd
    NGzip
    NGunzip
    NDeflate
    NInflate

    // **Images, which are `slate:image`.** Beside the encodings rather than among them, and for the
    // same reason they are not in `if everything`: these six ARE the module. A JavaScript host has
    // no decoder of any kind, so all six refuse there -- `slate:brotli`'s case rather than an
    // `OWED` stub's.
    NReadImage
    NImageShape
    NResizeImage
    NEncodePng
    NEncodeJpeg
    NEncodeWebp

    // **LMDB, which is `slate:lmdb` and the only store slate carries.** The names keep the library's
    // own prefix, which is `slate:llhttp`'s rule: this is a door onto somebody else's state machine
    // and there is no policy of slate's in it, so a session store or a rate-limit bucket is a
    // package written over these rather than another module here.
    NLmdbOpen
    NLmdbClose
    NLmdbInfo
    NLmdbRead
    NLmdbWrite
    NLmdbCommit
    NLmdbAbort
    NLmdbDb
    NLmdbGet
    NLmdbHas
    NLmdbPut
    NLmdbDelete
    NLmdbClear
    NLmdbStat
    NLmdbCursor
    NLmdbFirst
    NLmdbNext
    NLmdbSeek
    NLmdbCursorClose

    // **What `slate:sqlite` is written over**, inside `if everything` for the reason the RESP reader
    // below is: the connection, the statement and the row are C's, and everything a program touches
    // -- the statement cache, the transaction, the object with a `query` on it -- is slate.
    NSqliteOpen
    NSqliteClose
    NSqliteExec
    NSqlitePrepare
    NSqliteFinalize
    NSqliteQuery
    NSqliteRun
    NSqliteCompiled

    // **What `slate:redis` recognises RESP with**, inside `if everything` for the reason the HTTP
    // parser is: a reply's shape is slate's business and the byte scan is not part of the language.
    NRedisReader
    NRedisFeed
    NRedisTake
    NRedisCommand
    NRedisClose

    // **What the host this program is running on provides ITSELF**, and the six calls that reach the
    // one such thing a browser has and the interpreter does not. `hostHas` is the question a built-in
    // module asks before deciding which of two implementations of one surface to use; the rest are
    // the browser's `WebSocket`, which is a client a page can open where it has no socket at all.
    NHostHas
    NWsOpen
    NWsSend
    NWsSendBytes
    NWsOnMessage
    NWsOnClose
    NWsClose

    // Regular expressions, on PCRE2. **The names a regex answers to are its own** -- `NReReplace` is
    // not `NReplace` and `NReSplit` is not `NSplit`, because a method is looked up by the kind of its
    // receiver and the two do different things to different arguments. Sharing a variant would give
    // one diagnostic for two calls.
    NRegex
    NReTest
    NReFind
    NReFindAll
    NReReplace
    NReReplaceFirst
    NReSplit
    NRePattern
    NReFlags

    // What a declared type answers to, once it is a value. **`test` is deliberately the same word a
    // regular expression answers to**, both being the same question asked of the two things in slate
    // that describe a set of values -- and `mismatch` is the one a regular expression has no use for,
    // there being nothing inside a subject to point at.
    NShapeTest
    NShapeMismatch
    NShapeName

    // -- sets and maps --------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // **Each kind has its own natives rather than sharing an array's**, and the reason is the
    // checker rather than the machine. `methods_of` is one table for both, and the signature a name
    // carries is looked up by the native it maps to -- so a `forEach` shared with an array would
    // describe a set's call in an array's terms and complain about a program that runs.
    NSetNew
    NSetAdd
    NSetHas
    NSetDelete
    NSetClear
    NSetSize
    NSetValues
    NSetForEach

    NMapNew
    NMapSet
    NMapGet
    NMapHas
    NMapDelete
    NMapClear
    NMapSize
    NMapKeys
    NMapValues
    NMapEntries
    NMapForEach

    // **The weak pair, and neither shares a native with the strong one.** `get` on a map and `get`
    // on a weak map are two functions because the second refuses a key the first accepts, and a
    // shared native would have made the refusal a property of maps.
    NWeakMapNew
    NWeakMapSet
    NWeakMapGet
    NWeakMapHas
    NWeakMapDelete

    NWeakRefNew
    NWeakRefDeref
end NativeFn
